    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Los Olivos became part of the newly created district of San Martín de Porres in 1950, originally named Distrito Obrero Industrial 27 de Octubre.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://academia-lab.com/encyclopedia/los-olivos-district/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the mid‑1500s, the Lima Cabildo granted lands in the Chillón Valley to Nicolás de Ribera and to Francisco de Ampuero in Chuquitanta.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Olivos_District"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During Peru’s independence, Don José de San Martín founded the old district of Carabayllo in 1821, encompassing what is now Los Olivos.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://academia-lab.com/encyclopedia/los-olivos-district/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Los Olivos’ town hall sits 75 meters above sea level, offering a modest elevation over the surrounding plains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Olivos_District"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Los Olivos borders Puente Piedra to the north, Comas and Independencia to the east, and San Martín de Porres to the south and west.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Olivos_District"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Immigrants in Los Olivos bring diverse Andean traditions, reflected in local customs, food, music, and festivals.",
